\section{Exhaust a Small Width with an Integer Oracle}

\begin{verbatim}
for every four-bit binary pair (a,b):
    ua, ub = decode_unsigned(a), decode_unsigned(b)
    sa = ua - 16 if ua >= 8 else ua   # decode sign independently
    sb = ub - 16 if ub >= 8 else ub
    check decode_unsigned(u_exact) == ua + ub
    check decode_signed(s_exact) == sa + sb
    check carry_lost == (ua + ub) mod 16   # not the exact sum
    check s_overflow == ((sa + sb) outside [-8,7])
    check (s_less,u_less) == (sa < sb, ua < ub)
\end{verbatim}

Stimulate then sample after the combinational model settles. This oracle ranges over known binary patterns; it does not silently convert simulator X or Z to zero. Test meta-values separately if diagnostic behavior matters. Kasprzyk, Koch, Golze, and Rock's \emph{An Improved Intermediate Representation for Datapath Generation} notes that matching operator and variable widths to the data can optimize hardware. The proof obligation is to preserve all observed bits and flags, not merely the low result bits on familiar values.
